WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
AMOS: Welcome, everybody.

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.000
You can finish up your lunch and listen to
us.

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.100
I want to welcome you to today's Council on
Foreign Relations meeting on "The Future of

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.850
the Arab World: Youth Perceptions Looking
Forward."

00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:10.920
I'm Deb Amos with National Public Radio.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:14.969
I want to introduce two guests who will be
speaking today.

00:00:14.969 --> 00:00:20.850
Donald Baer, who is the worldwide chair and
executive officer of Burson-Marsteller, and

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:25.740
a member of the board of PBS, close to my
heart.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:33.809
Steven Cook, he is the-(laughs)-yes, the guy
in the box-Eni Enrico-I can't say all of these

00:00:33.809 --> 00:00:38.930
things-senior fellow for Middle East and African
Studies, Council on Foreign Relations.

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:40.930
And welcome to an on-the-record meeting.

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:46.300
We're going to talk about a very, very interesting
study.

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:53.660
And every year these studies always reveal
something new about the Arab world and its

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:59.800
youth, a population that is the majority in
almost every Arab country in the region.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:05.379
This is 3,500 face-to-face interviews, which
is unusual.

00:01:05.379 --> 00:01:11.590
It is with young people between the ages of
18 and 24 in 16 Arab countries.

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:20.000
And the attitudes that were revealed in this
study would be very much at home in the electoral

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.090
politics in this election.

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:24.680
In some ways, they are Trump voters.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:27.250
They are worried about ISIS.

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:28.830
They are worried about jobs.

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:31.299
And they value stability over democracy.

00:01:31.299 --> 00:01:35.090
That's not the only thing, but those are some
of the headlines in the study.

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:40.450
And so we're going to go through some of these
numbers so we can understand what these attitudes

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:41.450
are.

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:46.170
And I want to start with something that really
caught my attention when we were talking about

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:49.140
this study yesterday and deciding how to unpack
this.

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:57.670
And that's a question that was asked: Are
you skeptical about the role of traditional

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:01.729
religious values in your society?

00:02:01.729 --> 00:02:04.810
And the numbers are striking.

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:10.340
Let's talk a little bit about what the numbers
and why that might be.

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:11.340
Let's start with Don.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:12.340
BAER: Great.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:13.340
Thank you very much, Deborah.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:14.540
And thanks to all of you.

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:20.680
Yeah, the question, as it was put, was yes
or no, religious plays too big of a role in

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:22.060
the Middle East.

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:28.440
And overall, across all of the countries,
52 percent said, yes, religious plays too

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:30.120
big a role.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:34.920
Twenty-nine percent said they thought it didn't
play too big a role.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.739
They didn't say it played the right role,
but they were answering the question.

00:02:37.739 --> 00:02:41.440
And 19 percent said they didn't know or they
didn't really have an opinion.

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:42.910
So that's a pretty stunning number.

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:48.470
I mean, a majority of the Arab youth in this
category think that religious plays too big

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:49.470
a role.

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:53.060
But it's even more interesting when you begin
to break it down according to the different

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:54.150
countries in the region.

00:02:54.150 --> 00:03:01.800
So in the GCC, the Gulf state countries, 61
percent think it plays too big a role and

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:04.450
25 percent think that's not true.

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:07.569
In Levant and Yemen, 44 percent.

00:03:07.569 --> 00:03:14.170
So not quite a majority, but only 37 percent
said they don't think it plays too big a role.

00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:17.700
And in North Africa, 47 percent agree with
this statement.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:24.190
So it's pretty stunning across the region,
especially in light of what I think Americans

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:28.660
would-and others from other parts of the world
would think would be the attitude.

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:34.420
AMOS: And, Steven Cook, is it surprising to
you that in the Gulf that number is so high

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:36.540
among young people?

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:43.379
COOK: Well, it's not-in a variety of ways,
not all that surprising that so many young

00:03:43.379 --> 00:03:46.319
people believe that religious plays too much.

00:03:46.319 --> 00:03:51.720
First, the Gulf, of all the regions that make
up the broader Middle East, is perhaps the

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:53.710
most globalized.

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:59.830
The Gulf countries are becoming centers of
international commerce, logistics.

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:06.820
The young Emirates live among large numbers
of expats from all over the world.

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:10.650
The same thing among Qataris and elsewhere.

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:13.989
So I think there's-that factor is the first
thing.

00:04:13.989 --> 00:04:18.970
And then I think there's also a reaction given
what has been happening more broadly in the

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:24.220
region, whether it's the failure of the Muslim
Brotherhood experiment in governance in Egypt,

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:31.780
revulsion-as the survey points out-revulsion
at the interpretations of Islam coming from

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:37.430
the Islamic State and its leader, Abu Bakr
al-Baghdadi, has clearly had an impact on

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:43.699
the way in which Arab youth view religion
People have asked over and over again, what

00:04:43.699 --> 00:04:47.639
is the answer to the Islamic State problem
in the region.

00:04:47.639 --> 00:04:51.240
And what I keep saying is that the answer
really is already there.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:57.060
And you see it among young people and others
who have taken it upon themselves to counter

00:04:57.060 --> 00:05:02.580
the Islamic State's particular religious narrative.

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:05.030
And I think that's reflected in the survey.

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:15.970
AMOS: Don, another sort of headline for me
is that a majority do favor stability over

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:16.970
democracy.

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:20.960
I want you to talk a little bit about what
those numbers are and how they've changed

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:21.960
over time.

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:24.120
BAER: Well, it has changed over time.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:32.110
And they've moved away from, after the Arab
Spring, a sense that democracy was really

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:33.110
all that mattered.

00:05:33.110 --> 00:05:36.199
And you can see a very steady decline over
the five years of the survey.

00:05:36.199 --> 00:05:42.850
The good thing about this survey is that this
is the eighth annual ASDA Burson-Marsteller

00:05:42.850 --> 00:05:44.050
Arab Youth Survey.

00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:47.230
ASDA Burson-Marsteller is the name of our
Middle Eastern subsidiary.

00:05:47.230 --> 00:05:53.630
And I should say, the CEO there Sunil John,
who could not be here today, is the person

00:05:53.630 --> 00:05:57.560
who really pioneered this work and carried
it forward over those eight years with our

00:05:57.560 --> 00:06:01.800
great public opinion research firm, which
is called Penn Schoen Berland, which is part

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:04.569
of our larger firm.

00:06:04.569 --> 00:06:06.420
So you see that decline.

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:12.889
In fact, when asked the question: Was the
Arab Spring good for the Middle East-right,

00:06:12.889 --> 00:06:20.770
so in 2012, the first full year after the
Arab Spring uprising, 72 percent of all Arab

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:23.509
youth said, yes, it was a good thing for the
Middle East.

00:06:23.509 --> 00:06:29.410
This past year, this year, 2015 going into
2016, 36 percent.

00:06:29.410 --> 00:06:36.490
And that is steady really across the board,
except for in Egypt, where they still-a majority-or

00:06:36.490 --> 00:06:40.289
near majority still thinks the Arab Spring
was a positive thing.

00:06:40.289 --> 00:06:45.400
AMOS: Steven, can you unpack that number,
that Egyptians still think it was a good thing?

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:46.940
COOK: Yeah.

00:06:46.940 --> 00:06:54.260
Well, I'd like to look at the way in which
Egyptians are responding and the questions

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:58.190
asked, because the question is what Arab Spring
when it comes to Egypt?

00:06:58.190 --> 00:07:02.930
Remember, over the course of the last five
years we had the long transition from Mubarak

00:07:02.930 --> 00:07:09.930
to Morsi, the very difficult year of Morsi,
and then the return of the military and a

00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:16.120
military-backed political system under current
President Abdel Fattah el-Sisi.

00:07:16.120 --> 00:07:22.630
So it strikes me, given the kind of manufacturing
of support for Sisi, the kind of overwhelming

00:07:22.630 --> 00:07:28.400
effort on the part of the Egyptian government
to generate and maintain support for Sisi,

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:34.660
that it's possible that that number, that
61 percent number, reflects the fact that

00:07:34.660 --> 00:07:42.120
the government in Egypt has in fact fused
the Arab Spring-a term that I rarely use-the

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:49.400
uprising, the promise of the revolution and
the second revolution, on June 30th, which

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:56.319
is the coming to power of Abdel Fattah el-Sisi
into one broad category regarding the Arab

00:07:56.319 --> 00:07:57.319
Spring.

00:07:57.319 --> 00:08:02.699
And I think that despite whatever troubles
are happening in Egypt, there remains a significant

00:08:02.699 --> 00:08:04.669
amount of support for Abdel Fattah el-Sisi.

00:08:04.669 --> 00:08:11.139
At the same time, and this could also be a
function of that kind of anomalous-seeming

00:08:11.139 --> 00:08:17.060
number of 61 percent, is that there is a tremendous
amount of opposition to Sisi.

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:22.180
And when you hear voices coming from the region
saying don't give up on the Arab youth, the

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:30.720
revolutions aren't over, they're still unfolding,
that those people and many activists in Egypt

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:35.860
believe that the Arab Spring is not over and
this is still an unfolding story.

00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:40.469
And that they will, as they brought down Mubarak
and brought down Morsi, they will-they will

00:08:40.469 --> 00:08:46.850
forge a more democratic and open society,
even in-even in Sisi's Egypt.

00:08:46.850 --> 00:08:49.910
I think that it's-one second, Don.

00:08:49.910 --> 00:08:56.370
I think that that is something based on faith
rather than what we're actually seeing in

00:08:56.370 --> 00:09:00.910
Egypt, which is the institutionalization of
authoritarianism once again.

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:05.730
I think the most profound number here though
in the thing, if we can move away from Egypt,

00:09:05.730 --> 00:09:11.800
is that in Tunisia, the country that the media
often refers to as the Arab Spring success

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:20.670
story, totally erroneously, in that country
only 24 percent of youth polled believe that

00:09:20.670 --> 00:09:23.460
the Arab Spring had made things better.

00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:24.460
AMOS: Don.

00:09:24.460 --> 00:09:31.200
BAER: So I just wanted to say, I think this
forced question choice between stability and

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:34.029
democracy is a bit a false choice.

00:09:34.029 --> 00:09:40.560
And when you plumb deeper into the findings
here, you begin to see that the roots of democracy

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:45.240
and the freedom that it helps to engender
have become very strong.

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:50.660
I think another one of the striking findings
is that two thirds of the Arab youth, and

00:09:50.660 --> 00:09:55.980
it's pretty much the same for men and women,
think that more attention and support for

00:09:55.980 --> 00:10:01.089
human rights for women and personal freedoms
for everyone need to be part of what governments

00:10:01.089 --> 00:10:03.360
are helping to enforce.

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:11.150
So you begin to see the real roots of modernization
that are critically important, that the-what

00:10:11.150 --> 00:10:18.550
happened five, six years ago helped to unleash-now
being really-I mean, 68, 67 percent saying

00:10:18.550 --> 00:10:20.970
that they expect this now as a matter of course.

00:10:20.970 --> 00:10:25.339
AMOS: But there's some contradictions that
I see in the numbers.

00:10:25.339 --> 00:10:34.230
So the model country that young Arabs point
to is the Emirates, which offers stability,

00:10:34.230 --> 00:10:42.200
offers mobility-economic mobility, but is
certainly very short on freedom of speech,

00:10:42.200 --> 00:10:43.200
freedom of association.

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:49.360
You know, it's very tough, if you live there,
to have a political voice.

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:55.180
So how do you square this notion that they
want more freedom and they want more gender

00:10:55.180 --> 00:10:59.760
equality, yet the UAE-
BAER: I'm not sure that it's squareable.

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:03.700
But of course, the thing about the UAE that
they also point to-you know, there's another

00:11:03.700 --> 00:11:09.570
question in here about the role of economic
opportunity and jobs.

00:11:09.570 --> 00:11:17.000
It's a very acute issue that a lack of jobs
really is one of the top things that concerns

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:18.000
people.

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:25.300
Only in the Gulf States, and especially in
the UAE, do they say-68 percent of them-say

00:11:25.300 --> 00:11:28.130
that there are good job opportunities available
for them.

00:11:28.130 --> 00:11:33.060
So some of this is about if they feel that
they have what they need to build a good life

00:11:33.060 --> 00:11:39.170
economically, some of these other things perhaps
sort of fall to the background in terms of

00:11:39.170 --> 00:11:42.149
how they make the judgements about what the
ideal situation is.

00:11:42.149 --> 00:11:48.220
AMOS: Steven, is this still a restive generation,
or is this a generation, five years after

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:55.850
the Arab Spring, sort of curbing its ambitions
or its aspirations, if you just look at-

00:11:55.850 --> 00:11:56.870
COOK: I think it's both.

00:11:56.870 --> 00:11:57.870
I think it's both.

00:11:57.870 --> 00:12:00.350
I don't think we can make a broad generalization
about it.

00:12:00.350 --> 00:12:04.339
Although, let me-just going back to your previous
question and the previous issue about human

00:12:04.339 --> 00:12:09.430
rights and women's issues, I'm not at all
surprised by the large numbers in support

00:12:09.430 --> 00:12:13.970
of it, but I wonder how different that is
post-Arab uprisings than it was, for example,

00:12:13.970 --> 00:12:21.040
say, in 2008 and 2009, where the region was
quite restive and there were the first demands-or

00:12:21.040 --> 00:12:22.620
the first explicit demands.

00:12:22.620 --> 00:12:28.089
I mean, Arabs have been, you know, agitating
for representative government and dignity

00:12:28.089 --> 00:12:29.089
for quite some time.

00:12:29.089 --> 00:12:32.490
So that's a finding that, to me, is not entirely
surprising.

00:12:32.490 --> 00:12:37.970
Nor is it entirely surprising that people
look to the Emirates as a model, given the

00:12:37.970 --> 00:12:44.820
kind of-the overwhelming number of people
who, you know, cite economic opportunity for

00:12:44.820 --> 00:12:47.860
a variety of the political pathologies that
currently confront the region.

00:12:47.860 --> 00:12:52.540
But back to your question, Deb, specifically
to me about whether this is a restive generation.

00:12:52.540 --> 00:12:58.880
I think that in places like Egypt, in places
like Tunisia and others, there's been a certain-because

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:02.180
things have been so tough, there's been a
certain demobilization and depoliticization

00:13:02.180 --> 00:13:07.900
of people who would like to move on, they
want stability, they want economic opportunity.

00:13:07.900 --> 00:13:12.160
Some of these other things become-have become
less important to them.

00:13:12.160 --> 00:13:21.440
But there remains a core cadre of activists,
people who are not satisfied with that, that

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:28.529
they want-that they see their dignity not
just through a job, but also through their

00:13:28.529 --> 00:13:32.720
ability to process their grievances through
democratic institutions, their ability to

00:13:32.720 --> 00:13:39.120
express themselves freely, their ability to
shape their own societies and their own governments,

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:43.580
without the-you know, the kind of whims of
authoritarian political systems.

00:13:43.580 --> 00:13:49.490
So my-and the fact that the new authoritarians
of the region have not been able to impose

00:13:49.490 --> 00:13:55.310
their will on these groups I think sets us
up for a prolonged period of actual instability

00:13:55.310 --> 00:13:56.850
and uncertainty in these countries.

00:13:56.850 --> 00:13:58.290
AMOS: And I wanted to ask you, Don.

00:13:58.290 --> 00:14:03.480
This is, you know, a sequential snapshot.

00:14:03.480 --> 00:14:09.620
And we know that the differences over the
last five years are stability versus democracy.

00:14:09.620 --> 00:14:16.430
Are there other trends that you can see with
this generation that stand out in your mind

00:14:16.430 --> 00:14:17.430
in the numbers?

00:14:17.430 --> 00:14:22.949
BAER: Well, that's why I was going to the
data here.

00:14:22.949 --> 00:14:26.470
And I think it relates to everything we're
talking about and a sense of openness.

00:14:26.470 --> 00:14:30.279
So one of the things we asked them about was
their media consumption, right?

00:14:30.279 --> 00:14:33.139
Where, how, when, et cetera.

00:14:33.139 --> 00:14:35.000
So there's some very interesting statistics
there.

00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:39.889
And I don't have the longitudinal on this.

00:14:39.889 --> 00:14:46.420
But on a daily basis, more young Arabs get
their news online than from TV or print media.

00:14:46.420 --> 00:14:51.421
Now, that's not unlike young people in every
other part of the world, certainly in the

00:14:51.421 --> 00:14:52.829
United States.

00:14:52.829 --> 00:14:59.230
But it's a change because, of course, we know
that there's a great deal more permeability

00:14:59.230 --> 00:15:05.060
when it comes to online media and their ability
to seek and find what they're looking for,

00:15:05.060 --> 00:15:09.630
that does have the potential of opening their
minds and sort of creating a different kind

00:15:09.630 --> 00:15:10.630
of dynamic.

00:15:10.630 --> 00:15:14.400
Thirty-two percent of them read news online
daily.

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:17.060
Twenty-nine percent watch TV news channels
daily.

00:15:17.060 --> 00:15:22.649
And again, there are some international or
global news networks that have only recently

00:15:22.649 --> 00:15:24.860
been available to many of these markets.

00:15:24.860 --> 00:15:26.649
Only 7 percent read newspapers daily.

00:15:26.649 --> 00:15:33.580
And if you think about where governments have
the greatest opportunity to create a chokehold,

00:15:33.580 --> 00:15:38.889
if they choose to for censorship purposes,
newspapers are a lot easier for them to keep

00:15:38.889 --> 00:15:41.610
out of circulation, certainly than digital
media are.

00:15:41.610 --> 00:15:46.420
So that's an interesting, I think, development
and a trend line.

00:15:46.420 --> 00:15:54.570
AMOS: And is it, Steven, a moment where stability
versus democracy, it is more favorable, but

00:15:54.570 --> 00:15:59.310
do you see going forward that that remains,
because there's another interesting statistic,

00:15:59.310 --> 00:16:03.829
which is these young people still see the
U.S. as a strong ally.

00:16:03.829 --> 00:16:07.070
BAER: In fact, increasingly.

00:16:07.070 --> 00:16:15.190
They see the U.S.-I don't have the exact number-maybe
I do here-but 63 percent say they view the

00:16:15.190 --> 00:16:19.850
United States as an ally of their country,
versus only 32 percent who consider it to

00:16:19.850 --> 00:16:21.050
be an enemy of their country.

00:16:21.050 --> 00:16:24.850
Now, a third of them who view the United States
as an enemy is not a good number, but two

00:16:24.850 --> 00:16:27.690
thirds of them see the U.S. as an ally.

00:16:27.690 --> 00:16:32.570
AMOS: And does that square, Steven, with what
you see in the field?

00:16:32.570 --> 00:16:36.450
COOK: Well, I'm going out to the field in
about a week, so I'll let you know when I

00:16:36.450 --> 00:16:37.450
come back.

00:16:37.450 --> 00:16:38.450
(Laughter.)

00:16:38.450 --> 00:16:43.170
But I mean, when I talk to people, when I
see people, a lot of that is, of course, anecdotal.

00:16:43.170 --> 00:16:49.980
What I think is quite interesting-what some
of the numbers here reveal is that when you

00:16:49.980 --> 00:16:56.320
ask Arab youth, what do they think are the
biggest obstacles facing the Middle East,

00:16:56.320 --> 00:17:00.579
many of the things that they actually give
relatively lower priority to are things that

00:17:00.579 --> 00:17:04.260
have been obsessions of American foreign policy
for the better part of the last decade or

00:17:04.260 --> 00:17:05.280
more.

00:17:05.280 --> 00:17:13.380
Whether it's the lack of democracy, Arab-Israeli
conflict, even slow economic growth, which

00:17:13.380 --> 00:17:17.470
there's kind of-they're all over the map on
this question of slow economic growth.

00:17:17.470 --> 00:17:20.449
So the question is, how do they see the United
States as an ally?

00:17:20.449 --> 00:17:27.030
It's not a tremendous shock to me, given how
much time I've spent in the Arab world, that

00:17:27.030 --> 00:17:33.050
young Arabs respect the institutions of the
United States.

00:17:33.050 --> 00:17:37.300
They respect our form of government.

00:17:37.300 --> 00:17:44.240
They respect the kind of positive myths that
animate American society, as well as being

00:17:44.240 --> 00:17:46.630
champion consumers of American culture.

00:17:46.630 --> 00:17:51.641
So those things, I think, go to the idea that
the United States, despite, you know, what

00:17:51.641 --> 00:17:56.490
our policies have been in the region-and I
think the numbers for Iraq on this particular

00:17:56.490 --> 00:18:02.160
question are profound, how much the Iraqis
regard the United States as being an enemy.

00:18:02.160 --> 00:18:06.030
So if there's kind of a policy takeaway, if
it isn't obvious to anybody already, is let's

00:18:06.030 --> 00:18:08.400
not invade other countries and try to make
them democracies.

00:18:08.400 --> 00:18:09.400
(Laughter.)

00:18:09.400 --> 00:18:17.669
But it strikes me that the United States still
continues to have a certain magnetism to young

00:18:17.669 --> 00:18:22.100
people, precisely because of our institutions
and the way in which we believe we like to

00:18:22.100 --> 00:18:26.590
live, not because of the way in which we conduct
ourselves in the Middle East.

00:18:26.590 --> 00:18:33.651
AMOS: I want to move on to even a more recent
piece of-study that you have done, because

00:18:33.651 --> 00:18:40.380
I think it's revealing about American attitudes
as compared to the attitudes of this generation.

00:18:40.380 --> 00:18:43.740
I'm sorry we don't have a young Arab with
us, which would have been really quite nice

00:18:43.740 --> 00:18:45.200
in a study like this.

00:18:45.200 --> 00:18:46.940
BAER: That would not be me.

00:18:46.940 --> 00:18:50.929
AMOS: Let's talk a little bit about the new
study, where you look at American attitudes

00:18:50.929 --> 00:18:53.780
and ask some of the same questions, and get
some surprising answers.

00:18:53.780 --> 00:18:57.910
BAER: And let me go back a moment, because
in some ways we buried the lead, or one of

00:18:57.910 --> 00:19:04.320
the leads here, because there's a big finding
here about ISIS.

00:19:04.320 --> 00:19:10.289
Seventy-eight percent of these Arab youth
rule out any possibility of supporting ISIS,

00:19:10.289 --> 00:19:13.740
even if ISIS were to stop using so much violence.

00:19:13.740 --> 00:19:19.530
And that number, 78 percent, in the last year
is up from 60 percent who said the same thing.

00:19:19.530 --> 00:19:23.870
So there's been a dramatic movement away from
ISIS.

00:19:23.870 --> 00:19:30.770
Only 13 percent can say they can themselves
supporting ISIS, even if it changes its tactics.

00:19:30.770 --> 00:19:34.130
Again, that's down from 19 percent last year.

00:19:34.130 --> 00:19:38.289
So something is going on there about their
perceptions of ISIS.

00:19:38.289 --> 00:19:45.030
And for whatever its worth, 76 percent of
them say ISIS will fail to establish an Islamic

00:19:45.030 --> 00:19:49.960
state in the Arab world, and only 15 percent
think that that will happen.

00:19:49.960 --> 00:19:55.640
Now, we have done an additional piece of research-again,
our polling firm, Penn Schoen Berland, within

00:19:55.640 --> 00:20:00.809
the last week because we were interested to-especially
in the current environment that we're in-to

00:20:00.809 --> 00:20:08.100
contrast American's attitudes about Arabs
and Arab youth to what the Arab youth are

00:20:08.100 --> 00:20:10.170
telling us about themselves.

00:20:10.170 --> 00:20:15.900
So this is a general population survey, all
age groups in the United States, conducted

00:20:15.900 --> 00:20:18.330
between April 29th and May the 2nd.

00:20:18.330 --> 00:20:23.280
So remember what I just said about how they
regard ISIS.

00:20:23.280 --> 00:20:27.720
Most Americans-more Americans than young Arabs
are concerned about the rise of ISIS.

00:20:27.720 --> 00:20:33.840
So 85 percent of Americans are concerned,
including 52 percent who are very concerned.

00:20:33.840 --> 00:20:35.710
In the polling business, that's the top box.

00:20:35.710 --> 00:20:40.650
And that's a very intense number of people
who call themselves very concerned.

00:20:40.650 --> 00:20:45.470
In the Arab Youth Survey, 77 percent of Arab
youth are concerned.

00:20:45.470 --> 00:20:47.120
So they're kind of the same.

00:20:47.120 --> 00:20:48.850
It's interesting that the interests are aligned.

00:20:48.850 --> 00:20:53.980
Sure, there's more Americas, 85 percent, but
77 percent of the Arab youth are concerned,

00:20:53.980 --> 00:20:57.940
including 50 percent who are very concerned,
all right?

00:20:57.940 --> 00:21:03.740
Fifty-three percent of Americans think at
least half of young Arabs living in the Middle

00:21:03.740 --> 00:21:05.660
East support ISIS.

00:21:05.660 --> 00:21:11.740
Right, so 53 percent, a clear majority.

00:21:11.740 --> 00:21:18.250
And for whatever its worth, 48 percent of
Democrats think that, 51 percent of independents,

00:21:18.250 --> 00:21:21.660
and 60 percent of Republicans.

00:21:21.660 --> 00:21:25.320
But remember what I said about the findings
of the Arab Youth Survey.

00:21:25.320 --> 00:21:30.090
Only 13 percent see themselves as ever supporting
ISIS, no matter what they might do.

00:21:30.090 --> 00:21:36.289
So there's a huge gulf between what people
in this country think are the attitudes of

00:21:36.289 --> 00:21:40.700
Arab youth, and what Arab youth's attitudes
really are.

00:21:40.700 --> 00:21:43.160
More Americans believe ISIS will succeed.

00:21:43.160 --> 00:21:48.640
So remember, think about the sense of fear
that pervades here in the United States about

00:21:48.640 --> 00:21:50.660
ISIS.

00:21:50.660 --> 00:21:56.720
Thirty-seven percent of Americans think ISIS
will establish an Arab state.

00:21:56.720 --> 00:22:01.220
Thirty percent disagree with that, as compared
to what I said before, the 15 percent who

00:22:01.220 --> 00:22:02.429
think it could possibly succeed.

00:22:02.429 --> 00:22:04.909
Those are the Arab youth.

00:22:04.909 --> 00:22:10.100
Sixty-five percent of Americans think religious
plays too big a role in the Middle East.

00:22:10.100 --> 00:22:14.049
That's about the same amount, as we saw before,
of Arab youth who think that it plays too

00:22:14.049 --> 00:22:15.110
big a role.

00:22:15.110 --> 00:22:21.490
The point of all this is, there's a lot more
alignment around their views and our views

00:22:21.490 --> 00:22:28.409
of what's going on in that world, except when
it comes to this question of how much they

00:22:28.409 --> 00:22:29.770
support ISIS.

00:22:29.770 --> 00:22:35.850
And it just underscores, I think, the level
of fear and perhaps misunderstanding in this

00:22:35.850 --> 00:22:38.090
country about what's really going on in that
part of the world.

00:22:38.090 --> 00:22:42.179
AMOS: I also want to ask Steven, that's a
two-way street.

00:22:42.179 --> 00:22:52.120
Yes, Arab youth are huge consumers of American
comedy, American YouTube, American culture.

00:22:52.120 --> 00:22:59.919
But would they be surprised by those attitudes,
that Americans think that they all support

00:22:59.919 --> 00:23:00.919
ISIS?

00:23:00.919 --> 00:23:04.400
COOK: Actually, I don't think that they would
be surprised.

00:23:04.400 --> 00:23:09.900
I think they have quite a jaundiced view of
the way in which Americans view them.

00:23:09.900 --> 00:23:14.220
I think they have a fairly sophisticated understanding
of the kind of one-dimensional way in which

00:23:14.220 --> 00:23:19.510
the Arab world tends to be portrayed, whether
it is in the media, this election cycle, or

00:23:19.510 --> 00:23:22.390
in, you know, movies or television programs.

00:23:22.390 --> 00:23:25.430
I don't think that they would be at all surprised
by that.

00:23:25.430 --> 00:23:29.570
I think they think it's deeply unfair.

00:23:29.570 --> 00:23:36.659
I think the important point here, given the
way in which Americans view the threat, and

00:23:36.659 --> 00:23:42.230
the way in which Arabs view the threat, but
the differences in the way in which we perceive

00:23:42.230 --> 00:23:47.390
the reservoir or support for it, is a place
that is ripe for exploration.

00:23:47.390 --> 00:23:51.780
I think Americans need to be educated about
what's going on in the region.

00:23:51.780 --> 00:23:56.610
And Arabs need to be better educated about
what's happening here in the United States

00:23:56.610 --> 00:23:57.990
on that score.

00:23:57.990 --> 00:24:02.400
The problem is, is that here in Washington,
in particular, we'll take a look at these

00:24:02.400 --> 00:24:04.860
numbers and sit down and say, OK, great.

00:24:04.860 --> 00:24:07.960
What do we do about this?

00:24:07.960 --> 00:24:12.200
I would say there's more work to be done here
than there is actually there, because if these

00:24:12.200 --> 00:24:18.760
numbers are accurate, and they obviously are,
it suggests what those of us who've been saying

00:24:18.760 --> 00:24:25.700
that this is not a purely military fight with
the Islamic State, that that is obviously

00:24:25.700 --> 00:24:33.150
necessary, and that's necessary to degrade
ISIS and make it difficult for ISIS to do

00:24:33.150 --> 00:24:38.640
the kinds of things that it had been doing,
but that in order to defeat ISIS that has

00:24:38.640 --> 00:24:40.830
to come from within the Middle East.

00:24:40.830 --> 00:24:48.250
And these attitudes of Arab youth would suggest
that the Arab world is in a good position

00:24:48.250 --> 00:24:49.250
to do that.

00:24:49.250 --> 00:24:54.030
It may take some time, because even if there
are 15 percent or 13 percent who are supportive,

00:24:54.030 --> 00:24:56.179
that's still a rather large number of people.

00:24:56.179 --> 00:25:00.830
But the overwhelming number, the voices-the
cacophony of voices, the millions of flowers

00:25:00.830 --> 00:25:07.610
that are blooming in order to oppose this
ideology should be a source of comfort for

00:25:07.610 --> 00:25:08.679
the American people.

00:25:08.679 --> 00:25:15.320
But too often we are if not told directly,
it has been suggested that if we just dropped

00:25:15.320 --> 00:25:20.780
enough bombs on Raqqa, or Mosul, or wherever
these people may be found, that this will

00:25:20.780 --> 00:25:21.909
resolve the problem.

00:25:21.909 --> 00:25:22.909
It won't.

00:25:22.909 --> 00:25:26.450
Bombs are-people at the Council have probably
heard me say this before-bombs are efficient

00:25:26.450 --> 00:25:29.010
in killing people, but not sentiments and
ideology.

00:25:29.010 --> 00:25:33.659
And what you need is an overwhelming number
of people on the other side offering appealing

00:25:33.659 --> 00:25:36.169
counter-narratives to those of Abu Bakr al-Baghdadi.

00:25:36.169 --> 00:25:41.460
BAER: Yeah, and I-just to follow on that-I
think what all of this says at the end of

00:25:41.460 --> 00:25:48.130
the day is that there is great potential and
even possibility for optimism about what the

00:25:48.130 --> 00:25:55.190
future can hold, but that this is a generation
that is hanging in the balance.

00:25:55.190 --> 00:26:03.000
And if in fact they believe that the United
States and the West has fearful and negative

00:26:03.000 --> 00:26:07.090
attitudes about them, that balance can be
shifted in the wrong direction.

00:26:07.090 --> 00:26:14.320
But clearly there are trend lines here about
them wanting to move in the direction of modernization

00:26:14.320 --> 00:26:19.919
and openness and economic growth and opportunity-
AMOS: Gender equality.

00:26:19.919 --> 00:26:23.950
BAER: Gender equality, human rights, all of
those things that I think we would want them

00:26:23.950 --> 00:26:25.850
to be moving in the direction on.

00:26:25.850 --> 00:26:31.039
And so it is all the more important that we
educate ourselves fully and have a deeper

00:26:31.039 --> 00:26:38.270
understanding about what they really think,
as distinct from what our fearful estimations

00:26:38.270 --> 00:26:39.270
of what they think are.

00:26:39.270 --> 00:26:42.010
AMOS: That's why this is on the record.

00:26:42.010 --> 00:26:45.420
I'd like to open it up to questions from the
audience.

00:26:45.420 --> 00:26:50.750
And just state your name and brief questions.

00:26:50.750 --> 00:26:53.860
There and then in the back.

00:26:53.860 --> 00:26:55.110
Q: Thank you, Deb.

00:26:55.110 --> 00:26:56.460
Don, I'm Craig Charney.

00:26:56.460 --> 00:27:01.539
I used to be at Penn Schoen and Berland, but
like many people have fled it, including Penn

00:27:01.539 --> 00:27:05.120
Schoen and Berland themselves.

00:27:05.120 --> 00:27:11.419
You know, I'm particularly struck by the question
of the alignment of the results on U.S.-attitudes

00:27:11.419 --> 00:27:13.559
towards the U.S. with the Sunni-Shia divide.

00:27:13.559 --> 00:27:19.730
Because the thing that's striking for me as
I look at the findings is that it's essentially

00:27:19.730 --> 00:27:28.480
in the Shia-influenced world-Iraq, Lebanon,
Yemen, and Iran, of course-that the U.S. scores

00:27:28.480 --> 00:27:29.480
are low.

00:27:29.480 --> 00:27:33.210
On the other hand, they are remarkably high
compared to 10 years ago in the Sunni world.

00:27:33.210 --> 00:27:36.880
And Steven and I spent a lot of time worrying
about this about 10 years ago.

00:27:36.880 --> 00:27:41.950
And I'm very struck by there, at least, the
enemy of my enemy does seem to be my friend.

00:27:41.950 --> 00:27:45.730
I wonder if either of you would comment on
the seeming alignment of the religious divide

00:27:45.730 --> 00:27:48.659
with attitudes towards America.

00:27:48.659 --> 00:27:50.590
BAER: Steven?

00:27:50.590 --> 00:27:53.559
AMOS: We're going to throw this one to you,
Steven.

00:27:53.559 --> 00:27:55.520
COOK: Oh, I didn't hear you.

00:27:55.520 --> 00:27:56.520
I'm sorry.

00:27:56.520 --> 00:27:57.520
Hi, Craig.

00:27:57.520 --> 00:27:58.520
How are you?

00:27:58.520 --> 00:27:59.520
Good to see you.

00:27:59.520 --> 00:28:07.230
I think that it is clear that-I think your
last comment about the enemy of my enemy is

00:28:07.230 --> 00:28:12.010
my friend I think does have an impact on people.

00:28:12.010 --> 00:28:17.701
You do have a variety of Arab governments
and others who are looking to the United States

00:28:17.701 --> 00:28:21.669
for safety and for security and to provide
that security.

00:28:21.669 --> 00:28:26.200
There's a tremendous amount of complaints
around the region that the United States has

00:28:26.200 --> 00:28:30.970
not lived up to is promises in this area,
or leadership I think is the way in which

00:28:30.970 --> 00:28:31.970
they use it.

00:28:31.970 --> 00:28:33.890
And that's particular high in the Gulf.

00:28:33.890 --> 00:28:40.690
But to the extent that we are providing support,
the extent that the United States really is

00:28:40.690 --> 00:28:48.370
the actor that is combatting the Islamic State,
I think makes it-I think it makes it-it kind

00:28:48.370 --> 00:28:50.730
of reinforces the point.

00:28:50.730 --> 00:28:54.820
I wonder how long that will last, however.

00:28:54.820 --> 00:29:00.670
I think we've seen the way in which the United
States is viewed in the region waxing and

00:29:00.670 --> 00:29:04.309
waning over long periods of time.

00:29:04.309 --> 00:29:10.130
Now, on the Shia side, it doesn't strike me
as odd at all that young people regard the

00:29:10.130 --> 00:29:13.179
United States as an enemy.

00:29:13.179 --> 00:29:19.419
That's obviously-you know, you have messages
coming from Iran, efforts on the part of Iran

00:29:19.419 --> 00:29:23.100
to influence the region.

00:29:23.100 --> 00:29:27.840
There is a sense that the United States-and
I think it's on both sides-there's a sense

00:29:27.840 --> 00:29:32.000
that the United States has over-weighted its
support for Sunni regimes.

00:29:32.000 --> 00:29:35.450
And Sunni regimes are quite obviously hostile
to the Shia.

00:29:35.450 --> 00:29:41.470
If you-Saudi Arabia, throughout the Gulf,
there is a significant amount of hostility

00:29:41.470 --> 00:29:42.470
there.

00:29:42.470 --> 00:29:45.520
So, again, it's not entirely surprising to
me.

00:29:45.520 --> 00:29:52.130
And Iraq I think we should set aside because
I think across the board profoundly throughout

00:29:52.130 --> 00:30:00.690
Iraq, there is a sense of profound anger at
the United States, the-despite the trillions

00:30:00.690 --> 00:30:06.980
of dollars that the United States has spent
on Iraq, the millions of lives changed forever

00:30:06.980 --> 00:30:10.880
as a result of the invasion I think has a
profound effect on the way in which young

00:30:10.880 --> 00:30:12.760
people there view the United States.

00:30:12.760 --> 00:30:18.419
BAER: I'll just-let me just add, it goes without
saying that politics and attitudes related

00:30:18.419 --> 00:30:23.200
to political decisions are complicated in
the region, which is why I won't say it.

00:30:23.200 --> 00:30:29.890
But I think that we can't view any of this
without setting it against the context of

00:30:29.890 --> 00:30:38.929
the last 15 years, which has exacerbated many,
many of the problems, and which are unresolved

00:30:38.929 --> 00:30:40.399
at this point.

00:30:40.399 --> 00:30:42.539
And while we're not-well, they're just unresolved.

00:30:42.539 --> 00:30:49.720
And so I think that what you identified to
me comes back to why it's critically important

00:30:49.720 --> 00:30:55.770
that in this country we have a better, clearer
understanding of what is actually happening

00:30:55.770 --> 00:31:00.460
there and how different populations are approaching
these things in slightly different ways, or

00:31:00.460 --> 00:31:04.970
sometimes radically different ways, but that
we not lump it all together and also then

00:31:04.970 --> 00:31:06.760
have blind spots about what's happening.

00:31:06.760 --> 00:31:10.350
AMOS: But it's noteworthy it's a generational
attitude.

00:31:10.350 --> 00:31:11.350
These are young people.

00:31:11.350 --> 00:31:14.080
So that means it can be profound and long.

00:31:14.080 --> 00:31:19.080
But, two, can you see where those numbers
begin to go up in your study?

00:31:19.080 --> 00:31:21.030
So you've got eight years to look at.

00:31:21.030 --> 00:31:22.030
BAER: Right.

00:31:22.030 --> 00:31:24.110
And I don't have all of those eight years
to us.

00:31:24.110 --> 00:31:28.470
I mean, the one-the one trend line that we
have is not-well, whether it's a good one

00:31:28.470 --> 00:31:31.970
or not I don't know-is the question of was
the Arab Spring a good thing.

00:31:31.970 --> 00:31:38.340
And that is a very dramatic trend line, to
go from 72 percent to 36 percent.

00:31:38.340 --> 00:31:42.090
In terms of attitudes about the United States
and the like, I don't have that.

00:31:42.090 --> 00:31:47.730
But I think that it's a good question about
how things have changed over time with regard

00:31:47.730 --> 00:31:48.730
to that.

00:31:48.730 --> 00:31:54.029
The challenge, of course, in that is you might
not see the trend that you want because every

00:31:54.029 --> 00:31:57.090
year is a little different in terms of, you
know, what has been going on.

00:31:57.090 --> 00:32:01.070
AMOS: But it would be interesting to look
at those two numbers, disappointment with

00:32:01.070 --> 00:32:04.040
the Arab Spring and-
BAER: And the-yes, right.

00:32:04.040 --> 00:32:05.669
AMOS: And where the divide falls.

00:32:05.669 --> 00:32:12.809
BAER: One of the things we do see-it's not
the same-is this uptick in attitudes about

00:32:12.809 --> 00:32:17.570
the Emirates has tracked against the Arab
Spring decline.

00:32:17.570 --> 00:32:19.059
AMOS: As the model?

00:32:19.059 --> 00:32:20.190
BAER: Yes.

00:32:20.190 --> 00:32:23.019
AMOS: Question in the back?

00:32:23.019 --> 00:32:24.019
Yeah.

00:32:24.019 --> 00:32:26.970
Q: James Reinl from Al Jazeera.

00:32:26.970 --> 00:32:32.450
Thanks so much for sharing the details of
a very interesting report, which had a lot

00:32:32.450 --> 00:32:38.230
of information on how Arabs view America and
America's political process.

00:32:38.230 --> 00:32:42.840
And with that in mind, my question is about
how the current race for the White House is

00:32:42.840 --> 00:32:44.320
being viewed from over there.

00:32:44.320 --> 00:32:47.770
Over the last, you know, 24 hours we got a
pretty clear idea that in November it's going

00:32:47.770 --> 00:32:50.710
to be Hillary Clinton versus Donald Trump.

00:32:50.710 --> 00:32:55.600
Hillary Clinton's got a back record in the
Middle East, interventionist reflexes, think

00:32:55.600 --> 00:32:57.230
Iraq, Syria, Libya.

00:32:57.230 --> 00:33:02.960
Donald Trump speaks more about counterterrorism
and stability of nations.

00:33:02.960 --> 00:33:06.039
How do you think this is viewed from over
there?

00:33:06.039 --> 00:33:09.289
And do you think there might be any reason
for a preference one way or the other?

00:33:09.289 --> 00:33:12.350
BAER: So, again, Steven, I think you should
go first.

00:33:12.350 --> 00:33:14.890
I do have a point of view about that.

00:33:14.890 --> 00:33:20.320
COOK: Well, the last time I was in the region-(laughs)-everybody
wanted to know about Donald Trump.

00:33:20.320 --> 00:33:23.880
It was the conversation started.

00:33:23.880 --> 00:33:28.399
One of the reasons why I was in the region
was I was tired of hearing about Donald Trump.

00:33:28.399 --> 00:33:29.399
(Laughter.)

00:33:29.399 --> 00:33:40.100
But I think after so many invested so much
in what they thought President Obama represented

00:33:40.100 --> 00:33:51.940
and what he might do in the region, and their
hopes that were dashed, I think that largely

00:33:51.940 --> 00:33:56.220
Arabs-they may have preferences, but they
don't actually really believe that there's

00:33:56.220 --> 00:34:02.289
much of a difference between any presidential
candidate and what it means for them, and

00:34:02.289 --> 00:34:04.809
what it means for the core issues that they
confront.

00:34:04.809 --> 00:34:09.490
This is also-you know, we are an enormous
presence throughout the Middle East.

00:34:09.490 --> 00:34:18.570
But what I've been struck by in so many trips
back and forth after the uprisings was how

00:34:18.570 --> 00:34:23.920
profoundly inward the region is looking, and
how profoundly inward Arab youth are.

00:34:23.920 --> 00:34:28.909
Many of you know that I spent some time Tahrir
Square during the first uprising, January

00:34:28.909 --> 00:34:32.050
25th, in which, you know, the United States
was largely an afterthought.

00:34:32.050 --> 00:34:37.800
And I think a big story of the region-despite
these numbers about looking at the United

00:34:37.800 --> 00:34:43.110
States as an ally or not-I think a big story
is that Arabs-young Arabs have turned inwards

00:34:43.110 --> 00:34:48.470
and are search for solutions to their own
problems, and would like to write their own

00:34:48.470 --> 00:34:51.550
history, if I could be a little poetic about
it.

00:34:51.550 --> 00:34:53.870
They have tried different models.

00:34:53.870 --> 00:34:56.010
They have sought to borrow from different
places.

00:34:56.010 --> 00:35:03.620
And while, as they are-many of them are animated
by ideas of Western liberalism, there are

00:35:03.620 --> 00:35:08.160
those who are searching for authenticity and
their own way forward.

00:35:08.160 --> 00:35:13.210
So this is a long way of saying, yes, whoever
is the president of the United States is going

00:35:13.210 --> 00:35:21.950
to have an impact on their security, on their
own politics, and in some ways their own opportunities.

00:35:21.950 --> 00:35:27.020
But by and large, they don't see them as being
very different.

00:35:27.020 --> 00:35:28.020
AMOS: Don?

00:35:28.020 --> 00:35:32.810
BAER: Well, I think in the Arab world, as
in every other part of the world that I've

00:35:32.810 --> 00:35:39.240
been in in the last six to eight months, the
question of Donald Trump is the dominant question,

00:35:39.240 --> 00:35:45.270
or as I saw it-I was at Davos back in late
January, so not so long ago.

00:35:45.270 --> 00:35:50.140
And notwithstanding the fact that there were
many, many big issues for consideration-China's

00:35:50.140 --> 00:35:57.740
growth rate, refugee situation in Europe,
Brexit, any number of other things that might

00:35:57.740 --> 00:36:01.370
have been focused on, there were really only
three questions that the great and the good

00:36:01.370 --> 00:36:04.070
in Davos had that I could perceive.

00:36:04.070 --> 00:36:05.560
Why Donald Trump?

00:36:05.560 --> 00:36:06.740
Why Bernie Sanders?

00:36:06.740 --> 00:36:09.250
And why not Hillary Clinton?

00:36:09.250 --> 00:36:14.680
And so today I think that's kind of the same
set of questions that you see.

00:36:14.680 --> 00:36:22.540
I do think that this election has the potential
to be deeply destabilizing with regard to

00:36:22.540 --> 00:36:30.790
relationships with that part of the world
because of Mr. Trump's stated objectives and

00:36:30.790 --> 00:36:37.470
attitudes, and because of the great deal of
uncertainty that surrounds what a Trump presidency

00:36:37.470 --> 00:36:42.730
would be like for purposes of international
relations, global affairs.

00:36:42.730 --> 00:36:44.670
And again, I think it's true for the Arab
world.

00:36:44.670 --> 00:36:50.040
I think it's true for every other part of
the world.

00:36:50.040 --> 00:36:56.550
Q: Well, I'm Marlene Hess.

00:36:56.550 --> 00:36:57.973
And I'm chair-oh, stand up.

00:36:57.973 --> 00:36:58.973
AMOS: And wait for the mic, please.

00:36:58.973 --> 00:36:59.973
Q: Wait for the mic.

00:36:59.973 --> 00:37:00.973
AMOS: Thank you.

00:37:00.973 --> 00:37:01.973
Q: Thank you.

00:37:01.973 --> 00:37:02.973
I'm Marlene Hess.

00:37:02.973 --> 00:37:03.973
I'm chair of the International Women's Health
Coalition.

00:37:03.973 --> 00:37:09.550
I'm wondering if you could tell me how many
of the people that you surveyed were girls

00:37:09.550 --> 00:37:10.630
or women.

00:37:10.630 --> 00:37:15.540
And also, if you can comment at all, either
of you, on what's happening with youth and

00:37:15.540 --> 00:37:17.940
civil society in the region?

00:37:17.940 --> 00:37:23.910
I know some of our local partners in Egypt
and in Pakistan are being threatened now for

00:37:23.910 --> 00:37:24.910
their activism.

00:37:24.910 --> 00:37:28.350
So I'm wondering what you're seeing and what
you think for the future.

00:37:28.350 --> 00:37:34.000
BAER: Of the 3,500 people who were interviewed-and
by the way, these were all face-to-face interviews,

00:37:34.000 --> 00:37:40.240
not telephone, not Internet-this is a statistically
balanced survey.

00:37:40.240 --> 00:37:48.000
And so half of those who were interviewed
statistically were female.

00:37:48.000 --> 00:37:52.150
And in terms of the-I'm going to defer to
Steven on the question of civil society.

00:37:52.150 --> 00:37:54.970
AMOS: But one more detail I think was interesting
that you told us yesterday.

00:37:54.970 --> 00:37:56.210
These are urban interviews.

00:37:56.210 --> 00:38:00.170
BAER: So for the most part-and that is something
you have to take into account.

00:38:00.170 --> 00:38:05.270
So when we're doing face-to-face interviews,
they're more naturally going to be urban interviews,

00:38:05.270 --> 00:38:12.060
rather than those who live in non-urban settings,
which potentially has some influence on the

00:38:12.060 --> 00:38:16.030
direction of some of the numbers with regard
to human rights, and personal freedoms, and

00:38:16.030 --> 00:38:18.119
especially women's rights.

00:38:18.119 --> 00:38:22.950
So we don't know for sure what the attitude
would be in some of those parts of the world

00:38:22.950 --> 00:38:25.730
that don't have the urban touchpoints.

00:38:25.730 --> 00:38:29.329
AMOS: Steven, on the issue of activism?

00:38:29.329 --> 00:38:31.250
COOK: Yeah.

00:38:31.250 --> 00:38:32.990
Thanks for the terrific question, Marlene.

00:38:32.990 --> 00:38:40.970
I think that there is an authoritarian, I
don't want to say rollback, but there is an

00:38:40.970 --> 00:38:46.599
authoritarian rollback-(laughter)-in the region.

00:38:46.599 --> 00:38:51.510
And one of the-what's reflective of what is,
I think, what's happening in Egypt.

00:38:51.510 --> 00:38:55.470
A quarter to a third of the Arab world is
in Egypt.

00:38:55.470 --> 00:39:00.100
And when Egypt feels revolutionary, the region
feels revolutionary.

00:39:00.100 --> 00:39:04.540
When Egypt feels repressive, the region feels
kind of repressive.

00:39:04.540 --> 00:39:12.200
And I think that it's clear that the new old
regime in Egypt is doing everything possible

00:39:12.200 --> 00:39:19.940
using authoritarian measures to ensure that
something like the January 25th uprising never

00:39:19.940 --> 00:39:20.990
happens again.

00:39:20.990 --> 00:39:26.790
From their perspective, and from the coalition
of people who supported the coup d'�tat

00:39:26.790 --> 00:39:33.079
on July, 2013, the preceding few years were
an aberration, and that they were intent on

00:39:33.079 --> 00:39:35.640
resetting the natural order of things.

00:39:35.640 --> 00:39:41.140
That was not only about dismantling the Muslim
Brotherhood, but also dismantling those who

00:39:41.140 --> 00:39:44.150
were pushing for a more open and democratic
society.

00:39:44.150 --> 00:39:45.150
And that's Egypt.

00:39:45.150 --> 00:39:49.260
And I think that, you know, if you look at
the Gulf, which never really had a robust

00:39:49.260 --> 00:39:55.660
kind of civil society organization in the
way in which we think about it, you have a

00:39:55.660 --> 00:40:01.470
kind of profoundly repressive environment
in the region.

00:40:01.470 --> 00:40:04.730
One of the bright spots, however, is Tunisia.

00:40:04.730 --> 00:40:07.810
Although it's not the success story that people
have made it out to be, there is a thriving

00:40:07.810 --> 00:40:09.609
civil society sector in Tunisia.

00:40:09.609 --> 00:40:15.010
In fact, it's the sector where there's the
most job opportunity for young Tunisians,

00:40:15.010 --> 00:40:16.940
is in the civil society sector.

00:40:16.940 --> 00:40:22.620
But overall, throughout the region, civil
society organizations have found themselves

00:40:22.620 --> 00:40:23.620
under attack.

00:40:23.620 --> 00:40:28.350
And it should be-I think it's important to
point out in a place like Egypt, that does

00:40:28.350 --> 00:40:34.680
feel repressive, there were many civil society
organizations, including women's organizations,

00:40:34.680 --> 00:40:40.290
that welcomed the end of the Morsi presidency
and the return of the military establishing

00:40:40.290 --> 00:40:45.210
believing, erroneously, that they would reset
some sort of political transition.

00:40:45.210 --> 00:40:53.829
So to some extent, oddly, unfortunately, civil
society groups in Egypt in particular at first

00:40:53.829 --> 00:40:59.710
collaborated with the military and authoritarian
forces.

00:40:59.710 --> 00:41:05.650
BAER: One thing I want to say about-I think
you made reference to them being profoundly

00:41:05.650 --> 00:41:08.010
inward looking in that part of the world.

00:41:08.010 --> 00:41:14.090
I think we recognize that that part of the
world does not have a monopoly on that characteristic.

00:41:14.090 --> 00:41:15.090
(Laughter.)

00:41:15.090 --> 00:41:18.300
AMOS: Mr. Murphy-Ambassador Murphy.

00:41:18.300 --> 00:41:19.300
(Chuckles.)

00:41:19.300 --> 00:41:23.360
Then I'll come to you.

00:41:23.360 --> 00:41:29.200
Q: Richard Murphy, Middle East Institute.

00:41:29.200 --> 00:41:35.160
When your people conduct the surveys, they
are in a specific Arab country and the same

00:41:35.160 --> 00:41:37.480
team may move between.

00:41:37.480 --> 00:41:47.060
But my question is, on your list of questions
do you ask them to reflect on other countries

00:41:47.060 --> 00:41:49.839
other than their own?

00:41:49.839 --> 00:41:53.290
And do you have any conclusions about that?

00:41:53.290 --> 00:41:54.910
BAER: We do.

00:41:54.910 --> 00:41:57.290
And I don't have ready access to that.

00:41:57.290 --> 00:42:03.000
But we ask them to reflect across the board
on those countries that they think have the

00:42:03.000 --> 00:42:05.560
most opportunity.

00:42:05.560 --> 00:42:11.120
And the Gulf States are the ones that have
been the standouts, with the UAE-the Emirates

00:42:11.120 --> 00:42:14.690
at the top, and others as you sort of move
down through it.

00:42:14.690 --> 00:42:18.910
Now, those are their attitudes, even if they're
not in those countries or have never been

00:42:18.910 --> 00:42:21.770
in those countries, of what they think the
views are.

00:42:21.770 --> 00:42:30.150
So the GCC countries very much at the top,
as a rule, and then others as you move downward

00:42:30.150 --> 00:42:33.710
toward the bottom in terms of economic opportunity.

00:42:33.710 --> 00:42:41.359
Q: Ralph Buultjens, New York University.

00:42:41.359 --> 00:42:45.190
Two brief questions.

00:42:45.190 --> 00:42:48.720
You focused entirely on the Arab world.

00:42:48.720 --> 00:42:51.520
Are you extrapolating for the whole Middle
East from the Arab world?

00:42:51.520 --> 00:42:56.160
Have you made any attempts to look at Turkey
and Iran?

00:42:56.160 --> 00:42:57.220
First question.

00:42:57.220 --> 00:43:01.580
Second question is this: You're focusing on
the attitude of the United States.

00:43:01.580 --> 00:43:07.410
Have you explored the attitude to other external
powers, like Russia, Europe, and so on?

00:43:07.410 --> 00:43:08.410
BAER: Yes.

00:43:08.410 --> 00:43:11.280
So first we-this is the Arab world.

00:43:11.280 --> 00:43:14.390
It's specifically an Arab Youth Survey.

00:43:14.390 --> 00:43:18.580
Secondly-so some of those other parts of the
world are not included.

00:43:18.580 --> 00:43:23.940
And to your point about other countries, we've
spent time here talking about the United States.

00:43:23.940 --> 00:43:32.099
I'm going to find the other countries, if
they're in here, that they have commented

00:43:32.099 --> 00:43:33.510
upon.

00:43:33.510 --> 00:43:37.760
Why don't we-here.

00:43:37.760 --> 00:43:42.119
So this is-this is a reference to the previous
question.

00:43:42.119 --> 00:43:46.290
This is the country of which country-although
it relates to yours as well-for the fifth

00:43:46.290 --> 00:43:51.180
year running, Saudi Arabia is seen as the
top ally in the region, while Iran's influence

00:43:51.180 --> 00:43:52.390
is on the rise.

00:43:52.390 --> 00:43:56.680
So who would you say is your country's biggest
ally?

00:43:56.680 --> 00:44:04.040
So I'll just whip through this: Saudi Arabia,
31 percent; UAE, 28 percent; the U.S., 25

00:44:04.040 --> 00:44:05.040
percent.

00:44:05.040 --> 00:44:08.750
So the U.S. is the third-
Q: But outside the region-

00:44:08.750 --> 00:44:09.750
BAER: Yeah, I'm going to get there.

00:44:09.750 --> 00:44:17.950
Egypt, 15 percent; the U.K., 14 percent; Iran,
13 percent; Qatar, 12 percent; Kuwait, 12

00:44:17.950 --> 00:44:21.990
percent; France, 10 percent; and Turkey, 10
percent.

00:44:21.990 --> 00:44:23.260
We don't go into Russia.

00:44:23.260 --> 00:44:26.960
We don't go into China in this situation.

00:44:26.960 --> 00:44:30.130
But you do get some of these other countries
and you see where they are.

00:44:30.130 --> 00:44:36.480
But I mean, by pretty wide margins-I mean,
the U.S. is way, way ahead of any other Western

00:44:36.480 --> 00:44:37.480
country.

00:44:37.480 --> 00:44:42.160
AMOS: But the Saudis, who have only been at
this leadership position for the last year,

00:44:42.160 --> 00:44:47.370
it's already showing up in the survey among
youth in the region, which is interesting.

00:44:47.370 --> 00:44:48.370
BAER: Yes.

00:44:48.370 --> 00:44:51.230
But let me-there is some past data on that.

00:44:51.230 --> 00:44:57.599
So this year 31 percent for the Saudis.

00:44:57.599 --> 00:45:03.000
Number one, from 2012, has been the Saudis.

00:45:03.000 --> 00:45:09.690
Number two, last year, was the U.S. It's fallen
to number three this year.

00:45:09.690 --> 00:45:13.220
So it's toggled back and forth with the UAE.

00:45:13.220 --> 00:45:22.620
Number three-Qatar was number three up until
last year, when the UAE moved into that position.

00:45:22.620 --> 00:45:31.180
France was number five last year, and now
it's fallen down to, like, number eight.

00:45:31.180 --> 00:45:35.480
AMOS: But your question is a broader, how
do they see China, how do they see Russia?

00:45:35.480 --> 00:45:36.480
Do you have numbers on that?

00:45:36.480 --> 00:45:37.480
BAER: Right.

00:45:37.480 --> 00:45:38.480
I don't have that.

00:45:38.480 --> 00:45:39.480
AMOS: OK.

00:45:39.480 --> 00:45:40.480
COOK: Can I just add?

00:45:40.480 --> 00:45:42.140
I don't have anything on Russia and China,
but I just want to add, because there's other

00:45:42.140 --> 00:45:45.400
numbers that-these are Don's numbers and I
don't want to horn in him-but there's other

00:45:45.400 --> 00:45:46.400
numbers in the stuff that I have here.

00:45:46.400 --> 00:45:48.190
BAER: No, all numbers are welcome.

00:45:48.190 --> 00:45:49.190
(Laughter.)

00:45:49.190 --> 00:45:53.710
COOK: That I have here that come from the
study, that I think gets to part of what Ralph

00:45:53.710 --> 00:45:54.730
is asking.

00:45:54.730 --> 00:46:01.220
And that's which country would young Arabs
like to live?

00:46:01.220 --> 00:46:06.569
And the top five are the UAE, the U.S., Germany,
Saudi Arabia, and Canada.

00:46:06.569 --> 00:46:09.510
Which countries would you like your country
to emulate?

00:46:09.510 --> 00:46:17.150
The UAE, number one; the U.S., number two;
Germany, three; France, four; Britain, five.

00:46:17.150 --> 00:46:22.230
Interestingly, on both of those scores, just
for my own kind of interest, all of this discussion

00:46:22.230 --> 00:46:27.600
after the Arab uprisings about a Turkish model,
Turkey comes in pretty far behind these other

00:46:27.600 --> 00:46:28.600
countries.

00:46:28.600 --> 00:46:33.230
Only 7 percent want to live there, and only
8 percent want their countries to emulate.

00:46:33.230 --> 00:46:37.910
So it goes to show once again how far off
some of the ideas that we have gotten into

00:46:37.910 --> 00:46:45.780
our heads about the uprisings, what young
Arabs wanted after those very dramatic moments

00:46:45.780 --> 00:46:47.480
in 2011.

00:46:47.480 --> 00:46:51.109
AMOS: And then-yes, right there.

00:46:51.109 --> 00:46:52.970
Q: Hi.

00:46:52.970 --> 00:46:56.770
I'm Ethan Bronner with Bloomberg.

00:46:56.770 --> 00:47:01.770
I'm curious if you asked any questions about
not just their attitudes toward things, but

00:47:01.770 --> 00:47:05.310
their attitudes toward their ability to influence
anything.

00:47:05.310 --> 00:47:12.530
And one of the things that you-when they say,
for example, that their sense of the alliance-I

00:47:12.530 --> 00:47:16.420
can't tell whether they're referring to how
their personally feel or what the alliance

00:47:16.420 --> 00:47:20.650
with their governments, from which they may
feel, indeed, very alienated.

00:47:20.650 --> 00:47:22.130
BAER: Right.

00:47:22.130 --> 00:47:24.190
Which we don't know for sure.

00:47:24.190 --> 00:47:30.760
And that question is a very good one, about
the extent to which they feel empowered to

00:47:30.760 --> 00:47:32.590
influence things.

00:47:32.590 --> 00:47:34.510
That's not probed in this survey.

00:47:34.510 --> 00:47:40.670
And the question is a pretty direct one, you
know, which is an ally-or the best ally of

00:47:40.670 --> 00:47:42.569
your country.

00:47:42.569 --> 00:47:47.640
And so whether or not it means their government
or it means to the people of their country,

00:47:47.640 --> 00:47:49.020
we don't know the difference.

00:47:49.020 --> 00:47:50.960
AMOS: Those are important-that's an important
distinction.

00:47:50.960 --> 00:47:53.190
BAER: It's a very important distinction.

00:47:53.190 --> 00:47:54.190
AMOS: Yeah.

00:47:54.190 --> 00:47:56.359
Wait for the mic.

00:47:56.359 --> 00:48:00.849
Q: This is Amal from the Egyptian-American
Enterprise Fund.

00:48:00.849 --> 00:48:06.890
And I am an Arab youth, so I feel I relate
a lot of this data.

00:48:06.890 --> 00:48:07.890
(Laughter.)

00:48:07.890 --> 00:48:08.890
I can verify.

00:48:08.890 --> 00:48:13.631
My question is actually related on whether
you think perception of allies in the region

00:48:13.631 --> 00:48:16.010
is influenced by economic support.

00:48:16.010 --> 00:48:21.710
So I think in Egypt we did see Saudi and UAE
giving a lot of economic support to Egypt

00:48:21.710 --> 00:48:22.710
recently.

00:48:22.710 --> 00:48:28.390
And do you think-you mentioned before that
Washington should take more not just an external

00:48:28.390 --> 00:48:33.690
intervention and military aid, but also support-like,
empowering the people through maybe job creation

00:48:33.690 --> 00:48:34.690
and other programs.

00:48:34.690 --> 00:48:40.359
Do you think there's support to empower people
economically to tackle the region's problems

00:48:40.359 --> 00:48:44.280
from a-in a different way?

00:48:44.280 --> 00:48:45.849
AMOS: Steven?

00:48:45.849 --> 00:48:47.420
BAER: Steven?

00:48:47.420 --> 00:48:53.220
COOK: Just on the second question, I think
that this is something that we've been hearing

00:48:53.220 --> 00:49:01.900
for quite some time, about the importance
of the United States and others in helping

00:49:01.900 --> 00:49:03.069
create economic opportunity.

00:49:03.069 --> 00:49:07.050
That's one of the things that the Egyptian-American
Enterprise Fund is intended to do.

00:49:07.050 --> 00:49:12.559
And I think it's an important piece of the
puzzle.

00:49:12.559 --> 00:49:19.869
The question is, is that the only thing that
we should be doing?

00:49:19.869 --> 00:49:24.400
What lessons can we learn from the previous
10 years about what works and what doesn't

00:49:24.400 --> 00:49:25.420
work?

00:49:25.420 --> 00:49:31.059
And I think that if it was up to me, I would
say, look, I think we should invest in economic

00:49:31.059 --> 00:49:34.930
opportunity in the Arab world, because it's
actually something that we can do.

00:49:34.930 --> 00:49:40.400
When it comes to promoting democratic change
or, you know, giving Arabs a sense of, you

00:49:40.400 --> 00:49:44.210
know, dignity and vision, I think we are ill-equipped
to do that.

00:49:44.210 --> 00:49:47.790
Those are problems that need to be handled
within the Arab world.

00:49:47.790 --> 00:49:52.300
I think you make a great point, though, that,
you know, this sense of alliance is that-and

00:49:52.300 --> 00:49:57.530
particularly when it comes to Egypt, that
moment of crisis when Morsi's tenure came

00:49:57.530 --> 00:50:01.869
to an end and Egypt was sort of-the perception
that Egypt was teetering on the brink.

00:50:01.869 --> 00:50:08.340
And the United States was ambivalent, to say
the least, that the Saudis and the Emirates

00:50:08.340 --> 00:50:12.240
came to-came to Egypt's rescue.

00:50:12.240 --> 00:50:17.810
And that has an impact on the way in which-on
the way in which Egyptians view their allies.

00:50:17.810 --> 00:50:21.829
But I'll point out that there are a lot of
Egyptians who really liked the Qataris during

00:50:21.829 --> 00:50:24.040
the Morsi interregnum.

00:50:24.040 --> 00:50:29.339
And then as that era came to an end, there
was a lot of unhappiness with the Qataris.

00:50:29.339 --> 00:50:31.090
So I think these things can flip.

00:50:31.090 --> 00:50:42.530
I think overall, however, those who can marshal
the resources to help people realize their-you

00:50:42.530 --> 00:50:47.420
know, their economic dreams, their development,
you know, the idea of a secure job, and so

00:50:47.420 --> 00:50:50.270
on and so forth, is something positive.

00:50:50.270 --> 00:50:52.390
And I think people will look positively on
it.

00:50:52.390 --> 00:50:56.809
But the other piece of it, the other big piece
of it that the United States has been obsessing

00:50:56.809 --> 00:51:03.920
about, I think we're, as I said before, rather
ill-equipped to handle.

00:51:03.920 --> 00:51:08.099
AMOS: You right there.

00:51:08.099 --> 00:51:10.030
Q: Hi.

00:51:10.030 --> 00:51:11.280
My name is Ghassan El-Eid.

00:51:11.280 --> 00:51:15.849
I'm a professor of political science at Central
Connecticut State University.

00:51:15.849 --> 00:51:21.690
I'd like to follow up on the question of the
youth attitudes towards the United States.

00:51:21.690 --> 00:51:27.180
I think it's very important to distinguish
between government-to-government relationship

00:51:27.180 --> 00:51:31.640
versus people's perception-people to people
relationship.

00:51:31.640 --> 00:51:36.190
I was kind of surprised to see this favorable
view of the United States in the region.

00:51:36.190 --> 00:51:37.840
I'm originally from Lebanon.

00:51:37.840 --> 00:51:41.380
I go to the Middle East all the time.

00:51:41.380 --> 00:51:45.980
I would say that government-to-government-if
you look at Saudi Arabia or United Arab Emirates,

00:51:45.980 --> 00:51:48.190
for example, the relationship is close.

00:51:48.190 --> 00:51:54.440
But I would argue that people's attitudes
towards the United States is quite negative-is

00:51:54.440 --> 00:51:55.440
quite negative.

00:51:55.440 --> 00:52:01.960
The United States is perceived in the region
as a country that empowers dictatorships,

00:52:01.960 --> 00:52:07.309
is seen as a country that's an ally of Israel,
that's viewed, of course, in most of the Arab

00:52:07.309 --> 00:52:08.869
world as the enemy.

00:52:08.869 --> 00:52:12.680
So I want to point that out, that there's
a difference between government-to-government

00:52:12.680 --> 00:52:16.900
relationship and people to people relationship.

00:52:16.900 --> 00:52:18.680
Saudi Arabia is a case in point, of course.

00:52:18.680 --> 00:52:22.920
Fifteen out of 19 were from Saudi Arabia on
9/11, so.

00:52:22.920 --> 00:52:26.830
BAER: So, well, all I can do is cite the numbers.

00:52:26.830 --> 00:52:31.770
And let me go back to numbers that Steven
got to, which are very interesting.

00:52:31.770 --> 00:52:36.660
And one of the reasons why we asked the questions
the way we did was to try to tease to the

00:52:36.660 --> 00:52:43.140
surface personal feelings rather than what
you might assume would be some sort of a propaganda

00:52:43.140 --> 00:52:45.700
answer to the question.

00:52:45.700 --> 00:52:49.190
Which country in the world, if any, would
you like to live in?

00:52:49.190 --> 00:52:52.579
Any country in the world, open-ended.

00:52:52.579 --> 00:52:55.800
UAE was number one, 22 percent.

00:52:55.800 --> 00:52:59.270
The U.S. was number two at 15 percent.

00:52:59.270 --> 00:53:04.240
And from there it skates downward through
Germany, Saudi Arabia, Canada, France, U.K.,

00:53:04.240 --> 00:53:06.290
Turkey, Malaysia, and Kuwait.

00:53:06.290 --> 00:53:14.140
Now, last year it was exactly the same position:
UAE number one, U.S. number two.

00:53:14.140 --> 00:53:17.000
2014, it was exactly the same position.

00:53:17.000 --> 00:53:20.580
And I don't have the actual percentage numbers.

00:53:20.580 --> 00:53:30.400
2013, UAE has been number one across the board
for the last four years, but in 2013 and 2012

00:53:30.400 --> 00:53:33.550
France was number two and the U.S. was below
that.

00:53:33.550 --> 00:53:38.900
So the U.S.'s position, at least in this ranking,
has risen and sort of stayed steady for the

00:53:38.900 --> 00:53:43.190
last three years now, for whatever that's
worth.

00:53:43.190 --> 00:53:47.770
The other question which Steven made reference
to was: Which country in the world, if any,

00:53:47.770 --> 00:53:51.490
would you most like your country to be like,
all right?

00:53:51.490 --> 00:53:57.150
So 23 percent say the UAE, 19 percent say
the U.S., and again then it staggers downward

00:53:57.150 --> 00:53:59.829
from there.

00:53:59.829 --> 00:54:05.670
That has been the number one and number two
ranking-UAE and U.S.-for the last three years,

00:54:05.670 --> 00:54:07.810
2015, 2014.

00:54:07.810 --> 00:54:13.390
And again, the U.S., in 2013, was number three.

00:54:13.390 --> 00:54:15.590
France was number two.

00:54:15.590 --> 00:54:24.339
But in 2012, U.S. was not on the list.

00:54:24.339 --> 00:54:30.540
So I can't tell you why or exactly what is
happening, but there's a definite trend line

00:54:30.540 --> 00:54:35.010
towards a preference for the United States,
at least for purposes of these questions.

00:54:35.010 --> 00:54:37.630
AMOS: Yes, we could be as compared to what?

00:54:37.630 --> 00:54:38.710
COOK: Can I just-can I get on this?

00:54:38.710 --> 00:54:39.710
AMOS: Yes.

00:54:39.710 --> 00:54:40.710
BAER: As compared to living anywhere, right.

00:54:40.710 --> 00:54:45.420
COOK: Yeah, I think we need to kind of draw
these distinctions out a little bit sharper.

00:54:45.420 --> 00:54:50.150
And just anecdotally, anybody who's been in
the region will understand this.

00:54:50.150 --> 00:54:56.040
I'm quite used to being harangued about what
the United States has done where, who they

00:54:56.040 --> 00:54:58.610
support, what we've enabled.

00:54:58.610 --> 00:55:03.490
But at the same time, going back to what I
said to start, I think that Arab youth and

00:55:03.490 --> 00:55:08.710
Arabs more generally like the institutions
of the United States, the way in which we

00:55:08.710 --> 00:55:12.980
live, our popular culture, our high culture,
our low culture.

00:55:12.980 --> 00:55:17.549
After these harangues, I then say, well, you
know, where do you want to go to school?

00:55:17.549 --> 00:55:19.460
Oh, in the United States.

00:55:19.460 --> 00:55:22.880
You know, they want to talk about "Better
Call Saul."

00:55:22.880 --> 00:55:26.839
You know, a number of years ago, we were all-all
of us in the Middle East were watching "Community."

00:55:26.839 --> 00:55:33.300
We thought it was absolutely hilarious, because
people in Qom, the Iranian holy city, were

00:55:33.300 --> 00:55:38.020
passing around the DVDs of Da Ali Gi Show,
if all things.

00:55:38.020 --> 00:55:41.460
Now, Sacha Baron Cohen happens to be a British
Jew, but that doesn't matter.

00:55:41.460 --> 00:55:46.740
You know, that doesn't-I don't think-the idea
is that there is something, as I said before

00:55:46.740 --> 00:55:50.701
there's a kind of mystique and attraction
to both high and low kind of things that they

00:55:50.701 --> 00:55:52.569
associate with the United States.

00:55:52.569 --> 00:55:56.910
That doesn't translate, I think, into broad
support for American policy in the region.

00:55:56.910 --> 00:56:01.420
And that's why I think Ethan's question about
ally is an important one.

00:56:01.420 --> 00:56:05.760
And then I picked up on one, I have notes
here, about who do you regard as an enemy?

00:56:05.760 --> 00:56:11.140
And you know, I'm used to going to Egypt,
the place I go most often, and really taking

00:56:11.140 --> 00:56:13.400
it between the eyes about American foreign
policy.

00:56:13.400 --> 00:56:18.640
But nobody thinks the United States is an
enemy, per se.

00:56:18.640 --> 00:56:25.090
But they're unhappy with our conduct in the
region, while overall wanting to emulate the

00:56:25.090 --> 00:56:26.720
way in which we live here at home.

00:56:26.720 --> 00:56:31.650
I think things that outrage Arabs are the
gap between the way in which Americans live

00:56:31.650 --> 00:56:34.650
at home, or the way in which we would like
to believe we live at home, and our conduct

00:56:34.650 --> 00:56:35.650
in the world.

00:56:35.650 --> 00:56:37.980
And I think that's what the question is really
getting at.

00:56:37.980 --> 00:56:41.880
BAER: Well, let me get out on a limb here.

00:56:41.880 --> 00:56:48.130
I would speculate that what they dislike is
what they perceive to be our government's

00:56:48.130 --> 00:56:50.099
actions.

00:56:50.099 --> 00:56:57.590
But in fact, the affinity for our culture-popular,
high, low, whatever-they perceive more to

00:56:57.590 --> 00:57:01.579
be their affinity for the people of this country,
right?

00:57:01.579 --> 00:57:09.210
And this is where this election has some potential
to be very destabilizing, because depending

00:57:09.210 --> 00:57:14.240
on what happens if a majority of the people
in this country were to vote in a certain

00:57:14.240 --> 00:57:19.390
way it might send a very different signal
about not what the government alone, but what

00:57:19.390 --> 00:57:22.609
the population in this country, is thinking.

00:57:22.609 --> 00:57:27.369
Now, of course, as we know, election results
are always a lot more complicated than that,

00:57:27.369 --> 00:57:30.980
but nevertheless, that's where I think there's
some real room for concern, one of the areas

00:57:30.980 --> 00:57:33.580
that there's real room for concern right now.

00:57:33.580 --> 00:57:35.160
AMOS: Ayman.

00:57:35.160 --> 00:57:36.260
Not quite an Arab youth.

00:57:36.260 --> 00:57:37.260
Aged out.

00:57:37.260 --> 00:57:38.780
Q: Are you saying I'm not young anymore?

00:57:38.780 --> 00:57:39.780
(Laughter.)

00:57:39.780 --> 00:57:41.550
AMOS: Aren't you out of the 24-year old?

00:57:41.550 --> 00:57:43.220
BAER: That's a zinger here.

00:57:43.220 --> 00:57:44.220
(Laughter.)

00:57:44.220 --> 00:57:45.230
AMOS: He's older than 24.

00:57:45.230 --> 00:57:46.900
Q: Ayman Mohyeldin with MSNBC.

00:57:46.900 --> 00:57:50.830
I was curious to know if-you were talking
about the issue of stability and democracy.

00:57:50.830 --> 00:57:54.480
And this is always something that's always
debated in the Arab world, between having

00:57:54.480 --> 00:57:58.990
the plurality of authoritarian regimes or
if democracy opens the door to Islamist rulers

00:57:58.990 --> 00:58:01.089
so we must reject democracy.

00:58:01.089 --> 00:58:07.569
And I was curious to know if you had any data
on, for example, voter participation in countries

00:58:07.569 --> 00:58:11.990
like Egypt and Tunisia when there was the
perception, at least, of free and fair elections.

00:58:11.990 --> 00:58:17.260
So in my time in Egypt, at least through 2011,
the first constitutional referendum, the first

00:58:17.260 --> 00:58:21.849
parliamentary election, and subsequently the
first presidential election had a pretty substantial

00:58:21.849 --> 00:58:22.849
voter participation.

00:58:22.849 --> 00:58:27.410
I was wondering if you had any hard data on
that for Tunisia, to at least indicate to

00:58:27.410 --> 00:58:32.460
people whether or not their voting was an
indication of whether they preferred democracy

00:58:32.460 --> 00:58:36.960
over what they're getting now, which is complete
civil war in countries like Syria and authoritarian

00:58:36.960 --> 00:58:37.960
regimes, like in Egypt?

00:58:37.960 --> 00:58:38.960
BAER: Yeah.

00:58:38.960 --> 00:58:39.960
Great question.

00:58:39.960 --> 00:58:41.890
We don't have it here.

00:58:41.890 --> 00:58:43.859
And I don't know those data.

00:58:43.859 --> 00:58:49.970
But that's a really important question: Having
exercised their democratic rights, do they

00:58:49.970 --> 00:58:55.060
feel as though they still have not actually
had the impact and influence that they would

00:58:55.060 --> 00:58:56.130
expect to have?

00:58:56.130 --> 00:59:00.700
Have they become more cynical as a result
of having exercised the right, but not having

00:59:00.700 --> 00:59:02.760
seen the outcomes?

00:59:02.760 --> 00:59:07.319
By the way, again, another one of those questions
that you might turn back inward here and be

00:59:07.319 --> 00:59:08.450
asking similar questions.

00:59:08.450 --> 00:59:09.450
(Laughter.)

00:59:09.450 --> 00:59:15.579
AMOS: We are just about out of time, but I'd
like to ask, especially you, Don, if the reactions

00:59:15.579 --> 00:59:19.190
here tell you that there's other ways to ask
these questions, and some things that you

00:59:19.190 --> 00:59:20.680
need to know in number nine?

00:59:20.680 --> 00:59:22.230
BAER: We're always learning.

00:59:22.230 --> 00:59:26.590
And there's no question that there's a lot
more-there's a lot more rich, rich sort of

00:59:26.590 --> 00:59:27.840
data that we could be seeking.

00:59:27.840 --> 00:59:29.359
So, yeah, this is very helpful.

00:59:29.359 --> 00:59:34.390
And the only thing I would also say, though,
is these are direct, face-to-face interviews.

00:59:34.390 --> 00:59:37.619
You can only get so many questions in to play.

00:59:37.619 --> 00:59:42.440
And I for one am interested, and this is only
one person's view, that we do track some of

00:59:42.440 --> 00:59:43.720
these things over time.

00:59:43.720 --> 00:59:50.190
Even though they may be imperfect measures,
they are still measures of how attitudes are

00:59:50.190 --> 00:59:54.180
changing, and personal ones and not just,
if you will, canned attitudes that you would

00:59:54.180 --> 00:59:55.180
expect to get.

00:59:55.180 --> 00:59:57.790
AMOS: Thank you very much, Donald Baer and
Steven Cook.

00:59:57.790 --> 01:00:01.059
And thank all of you.

01:00:01.059 --> 01:00:03.030
BAER: Thank you.

01:00:03.030 --> 01:00:04.030
(Applause.)

01:00:04.030 --> 01:00:04.339
(END)

